exam: It is given that, were each child of a group to receive {p} candies, a surplus of {r} candies would remain, whereas, were each to receive {q} candies, a deficiency of {s} candies would arise. Determine the number of candies held by the teacher.
exam: Ascertain the number of apples in the possession of {a}, whereas the placing of {p} apples in each basket leaves {r} apples over, and the placing of {q} apples in each basket would require a further {s} apples.
exam: A stock of books is to be apportioned among a number of pupils. Whereas {p} books per pupil leaves a remainder of {r} books, and {q} books per pupil leaves a shortfall of {s} books, state the total number of books in the stock.
exam: Compute the total number of stickers to be distributed among the members of a club, given that an allocation of {p} per member results in {r} stickers in excess and an allocation of {q} per member results in {s} stickers wanting.
exercise: Grandma passed away and left a tin of sweets for her grandchildren. If each gets {p}, {r} are left over; if each gets {q}, {s} are missing. How many sweets were in the tin?
exercise: Since Dad was laid off, the family packs lunches for a school trip. With {p} sandwiches in each lunchbox, {r} are left over; with {q} in each, they are {s} short. How many sandwiches did they make?
test: teacher's 'having a moment' again. sharing sweets: {p} each n {r} left, {q} each n we're {s} short. how many sweets she got, come on, it's not rocket science
